A deadlock in MySQL occurs when two or more transactions block each other by holding locks on resources that the other transactions need.
 This leads to a situation where none of the transactions can proceed.

Example scenario:
Transaction A locks Row 1 and waits for Row 2.
Transaction B locks Row 2 and waits for Row 1.
Neither transaction can complete because each is waiting for the other to release its lock.

How to handle deadlocks:
Automatic rollback: MySQL automatically detects deadlocks and rolls back one of the transactions.
Best practices:
Ensure that transactions access resources in the same order.
Keep transactions short and simple.
Use LOCK IN SHARE MODE or FOR UPDATE selectively to minimize locking.

🔒 What is a Deadlock in Databases (MySQL) & Java?
A deadlock occurs when two or more processes/threads are waiting for each other to release resources — and none can proceed.
This causes the system to hang or stall until the deadlock is detected and resolved.

🔁 Deadlock in MySQL (Database Deadlock)
🧱 Example Scenario:

-- Thread A
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
-- waiting...
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;

-- Thread B
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
-- waiting...
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
➡️ Thread A locks row 1 and waits for row 2
➡️ Thread B locks row 2 and waits for row 1
🎯 Deadlock!

⚠️ MySQL Response
MySQL detects deadlock and throws:

ERROR 1213 (40001): Deadlock found when trying to get lock; try restarting transaction
✅ How to Avoid Deadlocks in SQL
| Tip                               | Explanation                                 |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Access tables/rows in same order  | Always update rows in consistent order      |
| Keep transactions short           | Don’t hold locks for long                   |
| Use low isolation level (if safe) | E.g., `READ COMMITTED` may reduce deadlocks |
| Add retry logic                   | Retry on error 1213                         |


🧵 Deadlock in Java Multithreading
🧱 Example Code:

public class DeadlockExample {
    static final Object lock1 = new Object();
    static final Object lock2 = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock1) {
                System.out.println("Thread A: holding lock1...");
                try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (Exception e) {}
                synchronized (lock2) {
                    System.out.println("Thread A: holding lock2...");
                }
            }
        }).start();

        new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock2) {
                System.out.println("Thread B: holding lock2...");
                try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (Exception e) {}
                synchronized (lock1) {
                    System.out.println("Thread B: holding lock1...");
                }
            }
        }).start();
    }
}
➡️ Both threads lock one resource and wait for the other.
🎯 Deadlock!

✅ How to Avoid Java Deadlocks
| Tip                                          | Description                               |
| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Lock in same order                           | Always acquire locks in a fixed sequence  |
| Use `tryLock()` with timeout (ReentrantLock) | Avoid indefinite waiting                  |
| Avoid nested locks                           | Keep synchronized blocks short & separate |
| Detect with thread dumps or profilers        | Use tools like VisualVM or JConsole       |


🧪 Summary
| Feature    | MySQL Deadlock                     | Java Deadlock                         |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Cause      | Two transactions waiting for locks | Two threads holding each other’s lock |
| Error      | MySQL error 1213                   | App hangs or freezes                  |
| Prevention | Lock order, short TXs, retries     | Lock order, timeouts, no nesting      |
| Detection  | MySQL logs                         | Thread dump, profilers                |
